"Home Tour/Resources - ZDesign At Home (https://www.zdesignathome.com/home-tour-and-resources/)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; From this page you can tour each room of our home as new tours becomes available.I have also included some of the more popular rooms from our previous home at the bottom, just in case for reference. ... Image: White modern kitchen tulip table zdesign\n# Home Tour/Resources\n\nWelcome to our homes!  From this page you can tour each room of our home as new tours becomes available.  I have also included some of the more popular rooms from our previous home at the bottom, just in case for reference.  If you have any questions whatsoever please don’t hesitate to drop me a line from the contact section of this website.  You can also shop any items you might be interested in below via the SOURCES links.  Thanks for stopping by and enjoy!\n\n## Primary Bedroom Tour & SourcesImage: transitional modern gorgeous bedroom with black leather bench from walmart home\n\n## Kitchen Tour & SourcesImage: modern farmhouse kitchen\n\n## Living Room Tour & Sources\nImage: gorgeous living room with black shiplap fireplace - modern farmhouse \n\n## Entryway  Tour & SourcesImage: entryway hall with cane console table decorated with large mirror, art and decor\n\n## Dining Room Tour & Sources Image\n\n## Laundry Room Tour & SourcesImage: elegant laundry room with gray green cabinets and light pouring in\n\n## Son’s room Tour & SourcesImage: teenage boys room design, long charcoal gray drapes/curtains\n\n## Guest Room Tour & SourcesImage: best guest bedroom ideas\n\n### Our Previous Home\n\nImage: White modern kitchen tulip table zdesign\n\nPrevious Kitchen Tour HERE\n\nImage: transitional neutral bedroom tour \n\nPrevious Bedroom Tour HERE\n\nImage: White-marble-Master-Bath\n\nPrevious Primary Bathroom Tour HERE--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBespoke Breakfast Cupboards | Chester Thomas (https://chesterthomas.com/bespoke-breakfast-cupboards)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Bespoke Breakfast CupboardsA breakfast cupboard hides the small appliances that otherwise sit permanently on a worktop, giving them their own working area that closes away completely once the morning routine is over.\n\n  1. Home\n  2. /Bespoke Kitchens\n  3. /Breakfast Cupboards\n\nImage: Olive green painted cabinetry with an oak-framed run behind, photographed part-way through a bespoke kitchen installation\n\nBespoke Kitchens\n\n# Bespoke Breakfast Cupboards\n\nA breakfast cupboard hides the small appliances that otherwise sit permanently on a worktop, giving them their own working area that closes away completely once the morning routine is over.\n\nBook a Design ConsultationView Our Work\n\n## Breakfast Cupboards, designed around the room\n\nMost kitchens have a corner of worktop that has quietly become permanent appliance storage — a coffee machine, a kettle, a toaster, and whatever cables and crumbs come with them. A breakfast cupboard is a deliberate response to that: a dedicated area, built with the right services and worktop for those appliances, that closes behind doors the rest of the time.\n\nInternal sockets are the practical foundation of the whole idea. Appliances can be plugged in permanently inside the cupboard, so there is no daily routine of pulling a machine forward, finding a socket and pushing it back again. Cable management within the cupboard keeps leads tidy rather than trailing across a worktop or dangling behind the unit.\n\nDoors matter as much as the interior. Pocket doors, which slide back into the cabinet body rather than swinging open, are a popular choice because they disappear when open and don't need clearance in front of the unit — useful in a kitchen where floor space near the area is tight. Bi-fold doors serve the same purpose where a pocket mechanism isn't practical, folding away neatly to one side rather than projecting into the room.\n\nInternal lighting makes the space properly usable rather than just a dark box, particularly if the cupboard sits under a wall unit with no natural light reaching it. Shelving above or beside the appliances holds cups, coffee, tea and everyday breakfast items, while a drawer below can take spoons, filters and the odds and ends that otherwise clutter a nearby drawer.\n\nThe worktop inside needs to withstand the same daily use as the rest of the kitchen — heat from a kettle, spills from a coffee machine, crumbs from a toaster — so it is specified properly rather than treated as a lesser surface simply because it sits behind a door.\n\nPart of our bespoke kitchens collection\n\n## What can be included\n\n  * 01\n\n### Internal sockets\n\nSockets built into the cupboard so appliances can stay plugged in permanently without a daily unplug-and-move routine.\n\n  * 02\n\n### Cable management\n\nRouting for appliance cables kept tidy within the cupboard rather than trailing across the worktop.\n\n  * 03\n\n### Pocket door mechanisms\n\nDoors that slide back into the cabinet body, disappearing when open and needing no clearance in front of the unit.\n\n  * 04\n\n### Bi-fold door options\n\nDoors that fold neatly to one side where a pocket mechanism is not practical for the space.\n\n  * 05\n\n### Internal lighting\n\nLighting fitted for a cupboard that may otherwise sit in shadow under a wall unit.\n\n  * 06\n\n### Dedicated worktop surface\n\nA worktop specified to withstand heat, spills and daily use, matched to or coordinated with the main kitchen surfaces.\n\n  * 07\n\n### Shelving for cups and provisions\n\nShelving positioned for everyday cups, tea, coffee and breakfast items close to where they are used.\n\n  * 08\n\n### Drawer storage below\n\nA drawer for spoons, filters, coffee pods or capsules kept close to the appliances themselves.\n\n  * 09\n\n### Ventilation for appliances\n\nAdequate airflow considered for any appliance that generates heat during use, such as a coffee machine.\n\nEvery commission begins with a conversation about the room, how it is used and what needs to be stored or shown.\n\nDiscuss Your Project07542 822456\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKitchen Tour - ZDesign At Home (https://www.zdesignathome.com/kitchen-tour/)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Image: white modern kitchen zdesignathome ... I think the first thing I remember telling our builder during the negotiation process of our home was that I wanted white shaker style cabinets (cabinet color is Decorator’s White by Benjamin Moore) and if he couldn’t do that for me then there was no deal! ... Call me crazy but at the time I just couldn’t fathom spending 35-40k on appliances when there was something else just as pretty and as functional out there for 1/3 the cost.\nImage: white modern kitchen zdesignathome\n\nImage: white kitchen carrara marble herringbone backsplash\n\nImage: modern white kitchen zdesignathome\n\nImage: white kitchen modern shaker cabinets RH pendants carrara marble\n\nGet the Look\n\nImage Turn on your JavaScript to view content\n\nImage Turn on your JavaScript to view content\n\nI think the first thing I remember telling our builder during the negotiation process of our home was that I wanted white shaker style cabinets (cabinet color is Decorator’s White by Benjamin Moore) and if he couldn’t do that for me then there was no deal!  The other major requirement I had for this space were the white Carrara marble countertops and a commercial stainless steel faucet, and everything else was secondary to those items.  The builder had actually planned on using Thermadore appliances in this home but I opted for the lesser expensive Bosch line to save money.  Call me crazy but at the time I just couldn’t fathom spending 35-40k on appliances when there was something else just as pretty and as functional out there for 1/3 the cost.  This decision saved us a ton of money of which I was able to use on other areas of the home to personalize things to make the home more us!\n\nImage: white kitchen farmhouse stainless sink commercial faucet shaker doors carrara marble\n\nImage: DSC_0193\n\nOne of the greatest things about this kitchen is all of the cabinet space.  I also love that we chose to put the microwave down in the island to give the upper cabinets a more seamless look.  Yes, it can be a pain bending down to access it but I love that it’s out of the way and doesn’t become an eye sore when you enter the space.  We were suppose to get a top load microwave but didn’t realize that until it was much too late in the process (insert miscommunication lesson between homeowner and builder here:) so I suppose there is always next time and for now this one works for us.  Another great thing about having the microwave in the island is that it’s much easier access for kiddos that are old enough to use it which can free you up by having one less job to do in the kitchen if you have some little willing helpers.\n\nImage: white kitchen island carrara marble counter tops herringbone backsplash modern shaker cabinets\n\nImage: white kitchen dough bowl carrara marble shaker white cabinets\n\nImage: oven stove white kitchen white carr\n\nImage: modern white kitchen coffee bar herringbone backsplash Carrara marble countertops\n\nImage: white kitchen carrara marble counter tops appliance cabinet\n\nAnd last but not least, two of the things that people always want to know about in our kitchen the most are the Carrara marble countertops and the herringbone backsplash so while I’ll have to cover those two items in a separate post in much greater detail later, I’ll speak briefly about them in this post and give you some resources below (UPDATE:  You can now see that post HERE).  The countertops are great and I absolutely love them but they do have their challenges in the kitchen.  In the bathrooms I have no trouble what so ever but in the kitchen you just need to know that they won’t always look like they did the day you installed them.  My builder didn’t want me to use them and tried to talk me out of them but I had my heart and mind set on Carrara and there was no talking me out of it!  I kind of hate that marble gets such a bad wrap because I think it’s so beautiful and versatile.  I know there are some quartz tops that are similar and they are lovely (I’ve used them for clients) but to me they will never be the same because marble just has a warm quality to it that can’t be matched. All of that said, if you are someone that can’t handle the maintenance and/or if you think a few character marks here and there will drive you nuts, I would definitely use another product.   I would however say the pros of marble far outweigh the cons and that they are not as bad as what you may have heard.  Seal them at least once a year, use a good daily cleaner/polisher, and wipe up spills as soon as possible and I think you’ll love them as much as I do.  I wouldn’t trade the decision to use them for anything in the world and would probably use them again if I had the chance.  The only thing I might do differently next time is to use a different surface on the center island where presumably most of the food prep goes on for most people (which is what I would recommend if you are on the fence).  And just like with anything else in life (and I’m pretty sure I’ve said this before) it takes a little work here and there if you want them to remain looking nice and I think the trade off of a little maintenance here and there for gorgeous white Carrara marble counter tops is more than worth it.  You just have to decide for yourself if you have the personality to deal with the quirkiness of this amazing natural stone.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2016 in Review- A look Back + exciting things ahead - ZDesign At Home (https://www.zdesignathome.com/2016-review-look-back-exciting-things-ahead/)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Image: ZDesign At Home - A Christmas Bedroom Tour ... If you are new here and haven’t taken the tour yet, I hope you will stay a while and visit the heart of our home right HERE.Image: pink alstromeria white tulips white kitchen flower arrangement\nClick HERE to see the full post.Image: 3-simple-tips-for-styling-the-kitchen-island\n\n#7 A Fresh Approach To Fall:  Here I shared a fun fall home tour where I shared my approach to fall using an unconventional color and accessories.  Go HERE to see the full post.Image: Harvest Haven fall home tour\n\n#6 Well Appointed Curtains: In this post I shared just about all you need to know to dress your windows properly and in style.  Click HERE to view the full post.Image: kravet riad linen custom curtains in silver how to know when to use what curtains west elm brass curtain rods-2\n\n#5 A Transitional Master Bath Tour: In this post I took you on a full tour of our white transitional en suite and shared several tips on how to create flow between the master bedroom and bathroom.  Click HERE to view the full post.Image: transitional white bathroom white cabinets carrara marble counter tops benjamin moore silver lake-2\n\n#4 Holiday Home Showcase Christmas Tour:  Here I joined some of my best blogging buddies on a special blog hop hosted by our dear friend Kelley Nan.  There was so much goodness on this tour with my buddies and I hope you’ll view it if you haven’t had a chance yet!Image: christmas-home-tour-white-slipcovered-couch-christmas-decor-transitoinal-livign-room-2\n\n#3 A Guest Room Retreat Tour:  In this post I took you on a tour of our cozy guest retreat and shared tips on how I pulled this space together with the use of fabric and key furniture pieces.  Click HERE to view the full post.Image: navy aqua farm style guest room\n\n#2 A Kitchen Backsplash Transformation:  In this post I shared how I chose the wrong back splash during the construction of our home and took you through the transformation of switching it out.  Click HERE to view the full post.Image: white carrara marble herringbone backsplash8\n\n#1 Kitchen Tour:  Drum roll pleases…and the kitchen no doubt was your favorite post!!  If you are new here and haven’t taken the tour yet, I hope you will stay a while and visit the heart of our home right HERE.Image: pink alstromeria white tulips white kitchen flower arrangement\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChurchill View | Architectural, Clive Christian (https://www.clivechristianfurniture.com/project/churchill-view/)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; A bespoke breakfast cupboard and pantry were meticulously designed, featuring integrated LED interior lighting and adorned with luxurious leather handles.\n\n  *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   *   * ## Key Features\n\n### Traditional style\n\nThe use of cabinetry from the Architectural Collection helped achieve a traditional design aesthetic, complete with columns and intricate decorative details.\n\n### Bespoke pantry\n\nA bespoke breakfast cupboard and pantry were meticulously designed, featuring integrated LED interior lighting and adorned with luxurious leather handles.\n\n### wine room\n\nA temperature-controlled wine room completes the kitchen, making a striking design statement while offering a cutting-edge solution for wine preservation and display. \n\n## Product Specifications\n\n### Cabinetry\n\n#### Collection\n\nArchitectural\n\n#### timber Exterior\n\nPainted Tulip & Cool Walnut\n\n#### Timber Interior\n\nCool Walnut\n\n### finishes\n\n#### paint\n\nAll White by Farrow & Ball\n\n#### handle\n\nAntique Brass\n\n#### Hardware\n\nAntique Brass\n\n#### Worktops\n\nCalacutta Gold by Levantina\n\n### special features\n\n#### appliances\n\nSub-Zero & Wolf, Perrin & Rowe\n\n#### Optional extra’s\n\nBespoke Walk in Wine Room, Breakfast Pantry, Stowaway Serving Trays and Marble Sink\n\nThis project was designed and delivered by Clive Christian Interiors Nantwich.\n\n## begin your design journey\n\nEvery project is entirely bespoke to each client, their lifestyle and their vision. Our expert design team are here to craft a space that will enhance your home and tailor it to perfection. Please contact us today, we look forward to exploring your ideas.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSummer Home Style in the Kitchen - ZDesign At Home (https://www.zdesignathome.com/summer-home-style-in-the-kitchen/)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; But I’m bringing in some summer decor to cool things off and brighten things up in our Kitchen, so welcome to my Summer Home Style in the Kitchen! ... I know so many of you have been waiting to see our full kitchen tour/reveal. ... Image: style open shelves in kitchen with white zellige tile backsplash\n# Summer Home Style in the Kitchen\n\n\nWe’re in full blown summer over here!  The days are long and needless to say it is HOT down here in Texas!  But I’m bringing in some summer decor to cool things off and brighten things up in our Kitchen, so welcome to my Summer Home Style in the Kitchen!\n\nImage: summer kitchen style and decor - how to style your kitchen island\n\n*This post contains affiliate links and was sponsored by Walmart, one of my favorite brands.  All opinions, photographs, styling and product selections are my own.  Please find my full disclosure policy here.  \n\nI know so many of you have been waiting to see our full kitchen tour/reveal.  We finally had our refrigerator door panels installed two weeks ago but the staining process still has to be completed.  It took them a bit to get the stain just right to match the other white oak cabinets and shelves in the space so now we’re just waiting on the final coat of varnish to finish it